{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"placid-dreary-zethuswasp","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Observation\nAfter Gerrit 3270 (7ea86184dac, disruptive-breezy-esok) treats an author's environment ids with non-overlapping time ranges as re-clones, the operator host's fsck reports no issues. The MCP server's own fsck (read through the MCP server, 2026-11-04 ~01:46 PST, after deploy of 4496e9acd2a) still reports env_id_mismatch: own env eea68b7f (its .env-id dates from 2026-08-26 PDT) against ad49f17f and b2b5c498, which nothing on the operator host identifies.\n\n## Hypotheses (unproven)\n- The server's ranges genuinely overlap: blue-green deploys run two containers against one shared tickets clone, so two environments can write in the same window.\n- The overlap rule's display-name matching pairs different writers who share a name across the server's environments.\n- ad49f17f / b2b5c498 belong to another writer (e.g. the review-bot clone or CI bridge) whose events reach the server clone.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] The two foreign environment ids are identified (which writer, which time range per author) from read-only evidence.\n- [ ] Either fsck stops reporting a benign case with a regression test, or the row is shown to be a real overlap and its message says what the operator should do.","id":"c4d9-7807-5c55-4b93","parent_id":"","priority":3,"tags":["ops-sweep"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Server fsck still reports ENV_ID_MISMATCH after the overlap rule"},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1793789721264666001,"uuid":"f3d02719-000e-41d2-a5ee-ecabe36d6b81"}